 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Charlie Constantinou is London-born and raised, but his family hails from Cyprus. Plans for a holiday there over the summer naturally piqued an interest in the sartorial traditions of the eastern Mediterranean isle—a curiosity easily sated thanks to The Cyprus Museum’s extensive digital archives. Specifically, he was interested in the 30-year transition period between the lapse of Ottoman rule at the close of the 19th century and the formal start of British colonial rule in 1925, a period in which “you see a lot of Ottoman traditions becoming Westernized,” Constantinou explained in a preview.</p><p>The most precise stylistic expressions of this period of cultural transition that Constantinou happened upon during his research were images of men pairing billowing linen vraka—traditional Ottoman pleated culottes—with tailored wool jackets and poplin shirting. This set something of a template for the designer, who presented riffs on the combination, as seen with the collection’s opening look, a trim, coffee-hued blazer with a notched sailor collar, worn with knife pleated bermudas in light charcoal linen.</p><p>On the womenswear front, pleat-fronted tunics and military shirts demonstrated perhaps the most confident articulation of the brand’s feminine ideal to date – a complement to the brand’s more robust menswear wardrobe, though with little sense of compromise. “With the womenswear, it’s the same idea as the men’s,” the designer explained “We’re not really preoccupied with gender—our clothes are very much for anyone who wants to wear them—but for the women&#x27;s, we’ve focused on recutting and focusing on body proportion,” which was evident in silhouettes clearly crafted to accentuate (if not skim) the figure.</p><p>While Constantinou’s particular historical interest made itself felt here, it should be noted (to his merit) that this didn’t land as a dry study of garments consigned to obsolescence, nor overly costume-y, as such archivally focused exercises often can. Thinking less in terms of cultural or geographic specificity, another key rumination of Constantinou’s this season, was “what the traditional dress of the future will look like,” he explained—what we’ll come to look back on with the same distant nostalgia that we look at, say, vraka now. Only time will tell whether Constantinou’s right, but at least his proposal here feels anchored in comprehensive research and sturdy conviction.</p></div>",
